<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Operation Bagration</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Belorussian strategic offensive · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Operation Bagration was the Soviet summer offensive of 1944 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, launched on 22–23 June. It destroyed most of the German Army Group Centre, one of the greatest defeats in German military history, and carried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> and the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> by August. It began about two weeks after the Allied landings in Normandy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/operation-bagration.jpg" alt="Operation Bagration" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United States Army · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BagrationMap2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By June 1944 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s Army Group Centre had been left exposed. Defeats of Army Group South at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and in the Dnieper-Carpathian and Crimean offensives, and the retreat of Army Group North, left its lines protruding towards the east. The German High Command expected the next Soviet blow to fall against Army Group North Ukraine under Field Marshal Walter Model, and it lacked the intelligence to discover the Soviets' true intentions. Soviet deception measures, known as maskirovka, sustained that mistake.</p><p>The Stavka committed about 1,670,300 combat and support personnel, 5,818 tanks and assault guns and 7,799 aircraft. Army Group Centre had 486,000 combat personnel, only 495 operational tanks and assault guns and almost no mobile reserves. On Hitler's orders its main towns, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitebsk">Vitebsk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> and Babruysk, were declared fortified towns to be held at all costs, which made the army vulnerable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Soviet partisans began attacking railways behind the German lines on 19 and 20 June, and the main offensive opened with an artillery bombardment of unprecedented scale. The assault broke through almost everywhere. By 28 June the German 4th Army had been destroyed, along with most of the 3rd Panzer and 9th Armies. Soviet tanks broke into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> in the early hours of 3 July, and the city was cleared on 4 July, trapping the German 4th Army and many remnants of the 9th Army.</p><p>The Stavka ordered the advance to continue far beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a>. Polotsk was cleared on 4 July, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> was reached by 7 July, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lublin">Lublin</a> fell on 24 July, Białystok on 27 July and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-france">Brest</a> on 28 July, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> came under Soviet control in early August. Soviet forces reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> and gained bridgeheads west of Sandomierz, and the German line along the Narew held until January 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> destroyed 28 divisions of Army Group Centre. The overall engagement is the largest defeat in German military history, with around 450,000 German casualties, and it set the stage for the isolation of 300,000 German soldiers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland-pocket">Courland Pocket</a>. It forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to fight on two major fronts for the first time, just over two weeks after Operation Overlord.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> used deep battle and maskirovka to their full extent for the first time, though with heavy losses, and reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>. In the reconquered lands it found cities destroyed, villages depopulated and much of the population killed or deported. About 57,000 German prisoners were paraded through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Operation Bagration” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Soviet <b>Operation Bagration</b> in June 1944 destroyed Army Group Centre in the same month as the Normandy landings.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…catastrophic losses, but the Red Army halted the Germans at Moscow in December 1941, destroyed an entire German army at Stalingrad in 1942–1943, won the tank battle at Kursk in July 1943 and smashed Army Group Centre in <b>Operation Bagration</b> in 1944, before taking Berlin in May 1945.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Military</a></li></ul></section>
